WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.059
The last two lessons made two promises about the generated plan.

2
00:00:07.059 --> 00:00:08.898
One: it uses no reflection.

3
00:00:08.898 --> 00:00:12.235
Two: one small edit doesn't redo the whole build.

4
00:00:12.467 --> 00:00:16.708
Reflection is a program inspecting its own classes while it runs.

5
00:00:16.708 --> 00:00:25.451
Part 4's container did exactly that: at the new house, every time the program started, it opened the boxes to read their sticky notes.

6
00:00:25.667 --> 00:00:28.602
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:28.602 --> 00:00:31.538
How would I actually know either promise is true?

8
00:00:31.538 --> 00:00:33.815
Do I just take the tool's word for it?

9
00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:36.405
No.

10
00:00:36.405 --> 00:00:39.619
Each promise gets a check you can run yourself.

11
00:00:39.619 --> 00:00:41.671
For the first, read the cards.

12
00:00:41.671 --> 00:00:44.269
For the second, read the helper's log.

13
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.302
The first check is a test.

14
00:00:47.302 --> 00:01:00.403
It reads every file KSP generated, and fails if any of them mentions reflection: java.lang.reflect, kotlin.reflect, Class.forName, and three more words like them.

15
00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:04.467
Here it is in Kotlin, with the same check in Java below it.

16
00:01:04.467 --> 00:01:05.701
It's a cheap habit.

17
00:01:05.701 --> 00:01:10.444
It fails on the day someone just quickly reads an annotation at run time.

18
00:01:10.444 --> 00:01:12.393
There's always a just quickly.

19
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:19.231
The cold-start race from earlier in this lesson agrees, from the other side.

20
00:01:19.231 --> 00:01:23.055
A JVM switch logged every class as it was loaded.

21
00:01:23.055 --> 00:01:26.020
The generated plan loaded 817 classes.

22
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:28.985
The reflection container loaded 1,059.

23
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.766
The generated side loaded no proxy classes, and none of the four annotation classes.

24
00:01:34.766 --> 00:01:40.861
Reflection made six proxy classes while it ran, to stand for the annotations it was reading.

25
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:49.494
The times jumped around on a busy machine, so treat them as rough: 25.5 milliseconds to wire the crew, against 71.9.

26
00:01:49.494 --> 00:01:51.601
The class counts never moved.

27
00:01:51.601 --> 00:01:56.468
The sticky notes were read once, the night before, and never again.

28
00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.069
Now the second promise.

29
00:01:59.069 --> 00:02:04.513
Incremental processing means KSP redoes only the files a change can affect.

30
00:02:04.513 --> 00:02:12.061
In the moving-house picture, you repack one box next weekend, so you redo its card, and the master list.

31
00:02:12.061 --> 00:02:13.149
Not every card.

32
00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:18.797
To know which cards, the helper keeps a log of whose label he looked at while writing each one.

33
00:02:18.797 --> 00:02:23.769
Change the truck's label, and everything written after looking at it gets another look.

34
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:25.780
Let's test it.

35
00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:28.712
Build once with KSP's log switched on.

36
00:02:28.712 --> 00:02:32.955
Then add one comment line to Truck.kt, and build again.

37
00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:35.698
Lesson 3's source set has 17 files.

38
00:02:35.698 --> 00:02:40.327
How many does KSP mark as dirty, meaning they need another look?

39
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:44.485
Five.

40
00:02:44.485 --> 00:02:48.173
First Truck.kt, because we changed it.

41
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:55.873
Then Crew.kt and MovingDayComponent.kt, because the processors looked up Truck while reading them.

42
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.022
Then MovingDay.kt, which looked up Crew.

43
00:02:59.022 --> 00:03:02.748
The dirt spreads like that until no new file joins.

44
00:03:02.967 --> 00:03:06.015
And RouteModule.kt, for another reason.

45
00:03:06.015 --> 00:03:13.207
It's a declared source of the component, the aggregating master list that any change redoes.

46
00:03:13.207 --> 00:03:16.256
Five of 17: the log says 29.41 percent.

47
00:03:16.867 --> 00:03:17.868
Now the cards.

48
00:03:17.868 --> 00:03:23.804
TruckFactory, CrewFactory and MovingDayFactory are redone, and so is the component.

49
00:03:23.804 --> 00:03:31.671
The other four, for the driver, the GPS, the packer and the tape roll, come back from KSP's backup, untouched.

50
00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:35.578
As far as those four cards know, nothing happened this weekend.

51
00:03:35.578 --> 00:03:39.081
That's what writing down where each card came from buys you.

52
00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:42.813
Two traps if you try this yourself.

53
00:03:42.813 --> 00:03:49.035
touch Truck.kt changes nothing: it changes the file's date, and Gradle compares the text.

54
00:03:49.035 --> 00:03:51.692
A pretend edit gets a pretend rebuild.

55
00:03:51.692 --> 00:03:59.871
And the build cache can bring back old outputs after you undo an edit, so make a fresh edit, or add --no-build-cache.

56
00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:01.557
And one honest oddity.

57
00:04:01.557 --> 00:04:07.252
In the lesson's three tries, one run did a full pass, with no file listed as modified.

58
00:04:07.252 --> 00:04:09.967
The lesson didn't find what triggered it.

59
00:04:09.967 --> 00:04:12.351
The next edit was incremental again.

60
00:04:12.567 --> 00:04:15.702
That's also the limit of the aggregating design.

61
00:04:15.702 --> 00:04:19.164
The component is redone on every edit, however small.

62
00:04:19.164 --> 00:04:20.797
Here it's one short file.

63
00:04:20.797 --> 00:04:27.591
With hundreds of classes, it's one long file every time: a fair price for a plan that's always complete.

64
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:29.769
So how do you know?

65
00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:31.840
You don't take the tool's word.

66
00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:38.519
Test the generated text for reflection, count the classes it loads, and read the log after one edit.

67
00:04:38.519 --> 00:04:39.855
Proof, not promises.

68
00:04:40.067 --> 00:04:40.986
Quick check.

69
00:04:40.986 --> 00:04:44.511
This time, add a comment to Packer.kt instead.

70
00:04:44.511 --> 00:04:46.273
Which cards are redone?

71
00:04:49.467 --> 00:04:54.033
PackerFactory, CrewFactory, MovingDayFactory, and the component.

72
00:04:54.033 --> 00:04:58.385
The crew looked up Packer, and the moving day looked up Crew.

73
00:04:58.385 --> 00:04:59.812
29.41 percent again.
